W1.2g evidence index — socket supervisor notification backlog

Scope: crates/bbt-supervisor/src/client.rs and its new client/tests.rs;
crates/bbt-mux/tests/socket_backlog.rs is the real-logic regression. No production
logic, routing, ring, writer, identity, protocol, dependency or runtime edits.

Contract: Vesper af756151 (10,000 requests, byte-exact replay and exit) and
b8116fe0 (successful AttachRing only; raw/pre-ring FIFO keeps the named 1024
failure; bound is distinct pending seat+generation+kind; retain lifecycle).
Base was current main f248c6b, including accepted W1.2d 86384b5.

Mechanism:
- client.rs:58 PendingEvents keeps ordered delivery plus a slot index. Only
  ring-backed Output/Resized replace their previous slot; replacement moves to
  the back so an intervening resize is delivered before later output. Exited
  events are never replaced. Generations have independent keys.
- client.rs:89 promotes only the confirmed ring's covered sequence range.
  Pending notifications can precede their attach reply in the same read, but
  pre-ring records remain byte-for-byte FIFO. Raw backlog retains its own limit.
- client.rs:177 confirms only a successful AttachRing reply matching exact
  seat+generation; raw Attach and failed AttachRing never authorize coalescing.
- client/tests.rs covers pre-ring bytes, exact generation, raw bound, resize
  ordering and 500 retained generations (1500 slots), plus 10,000 real socket
  requests without receive, with raw/failed attach checks before confirmation.
- socket_backlog.rs runs a real PTY and supervisor socket with explicit fixture
  signing material and a private 0700 temp root. Logic issues 10,000 List requests
  while a deterministic producer writes at full rate, replays coalesced spans,
  observes exit, verifies viewport tail/sequence and reads all capture records
  with contiguous sequences and an independent expected SHA256.

Development commands/results (before the exact final clean-worktree gates):
- w1-2g-before.log: initial fixture import compilation 101, corrected to
  protocol::ExitInfo; not a demonstrated behavior failure.
- w1-2g-before-02.log: baseline original client compiled, then failed 101 with
  'supervisor event backlog exceeded; continuity unavailable'.
- w1-2g-client-01.log: first coalesced 10,000-request test exit 0.
- w1-2g-client-02.log: three boundary cases passed, mock socket returned EINVAL
  with the peer closing immediately after its last reply. The fixture now keeps
  the peer alive until client cleanup, with bounded socket timeouts and a joined
  thread; production socket disconnect handling/timeouts are unchanged.
- w1-2g-client-03.log: all four boundary/socket tests exit 0.
- w1-2g-client-repeat-final.log: final fixture, ten default-parallel runs,
  40 cases, all child exits 0. Earlier repeat evidence also retained.
- w1-2g-real-pty-01.log: fixture startup 101 because tempdir mode was not 0700.
  Fixed the owned fixture root, leaving strict production socket checks intact.
- w1-2g-real-pty-02.log: real PTY test exit 0, 10,000 requests in 6.26991525 s,
  exact 67,108,877 bytes (64 MiB plus tail), exit sequence 4358, 75.11 s total.
  The early log's replayed_records includes the later manual history check;
  final fixture logs logic_replayed_records before that manual verification.
- w1-2g-preflight.log: supervisor tests (18 unit + 2 journal + 10 PTY passed,
  2 fixture units ignored), capture_busy 2/2, scoped strict all-target Clippy
  and fmt checks all exit 0. No second Tokio runtime or new dependency.

The final 967-seat staged gate and ci-local.sh run occur in a detached clean
worktree of the signed handoff SHA. Their actual SHA, commands, child exits,
timings, measurements and cleanup are recorded in separate w1-2g final logs.
This pre-gate snapshot does not claim those subsequent results.

Limits: raw-only overflow remains an explicit failure by ruling. Discarded
captured spans require retained ring history; an actual ring gap remains an
explicit error. Exit is one event per process generation; pending generations
remain independent until consumed. No production keychain or real model used.
No new W1.2f throughput or W1.2e reaping claim. No physical power loss or new
macOS14/Linux/remote-provider coverage. No browser opened, no peer paths/PIDs
touched. W2.5b remains parked.
